Stop reading `UINTPTR_MAX == UINT64_MAX` as "a pointer is 8 bytes".

The EMS allocator decides its entire heap layout from that predicate. It is used
as a stand-in for the pointer's width, and on a target where a pointer carries
base, cursor, end and a tag while uintptr_t holds only the address, it is true and
wrong at the same time. Everything downstream then takes the 8-byte-pointer
branch:

  * hmu_tree_node_t is declared __attribute__((packed)) so that a 4-byte header
    plus three pointers leaves the pointer fields 8-aligned. With 16-byte pointers
    they land at 4, 20 and 36, none of them aligned, and `packed` additionally
    declares them align-1. A capability cannot survive an align-1 access.
  * gc_heap_t carries a uint32 __padding whose only job is to push the embedded
    root node off 8-alignment, for the same arithmetic.
  * GC_ALIGN_8 and GC_MIN_ALIGNMENT step the heap in units of 8.
  * GC_HEAD_PADDING is 4 so that hmu + HMU_SIZE is 8-aligned.

None of this can be caught by the guards that are there. bh_static_assert(sizeof
== 8 + 3 * sizeof(void *)) evaluates 56 == 56 and passes; offsetof(left) == 4
passes; ASSERT_TREE_NODE_ALIGNED_ACCESS checks & (sizeof(uintptr_t) - 1), which is
& 7 on this target, so it waves a 16-byte access through.

The fix asks the question the code means, using __SIZEOF_POINTER__, and lets the
layout follow: the node is not packed, the padding is gone, the heap steps in
units of sizeof(void *), and GC_HEAD_PADDING becomes sizeof(void *) - HMU_SIZE so
that every OBJECT handed to a caller is aligned for the pointers it will hold.
The static assert is replaced by one that checks what actually matters, that each
pointer field is naturally aligned.

Also here, and separately real: alloc_hmu returned its tree-path result through a
uintptr_t local, which keeps the address and drops the rest. On a fresh heap the
normal free lists are empty, so the scan falls through to the tree and EVERY
allocation takes that path. The small-block path a few lines above already returns
the pointer directly.

And gc_init_with_pool aligned its pool to 8 rather than to the pointer's
alignment, which everything carved out of it inherits.

VERIFIED IN THE GENERATED CODE, not inferred: before, alloc_hmu_ex read the node's
size with `lw a4, 0x34(s1)` and assembled the pointers by hand; after, it is
`ldc a2, 0x10(s1)` and `ldc s1, 0x20(s1)` -- capability loads at the natural
offsets of an unpacked node.

AND THE ALIGNMENT HAS TWO MASTERS, which the first attempt got wrong. A free TREE
NODE sits at the START of a chunk -- gc_init_internal does
`q = (hmu_tree_node_t *)heap->base_addr` -- so chunks must be pointer-aligned. An
OBJECT sits at hmu + HMU_SIZE + OBJ_PREFIX_SIZE, and with HMU_SIZE 4 and no prefix
those two demands are 4 bytes apart and cannot both hold.

Padding the CHUNK to fix the object side aligns objects and misaligns every tree
node, which is strictly worse: nodes are written during heap init, before anything
is allocated. So the padding is 0 and the gap is closed on the object side, by
giving OBJ_PREFIX_SIZE a value on this target. Both then land on sizeof(void *).

